<p>Calendar 2024 data shows the UAE and Bangladesh essentially tied for India's largest turmeric market — UAE at USD 50.9 million against Bangladesh's USD 50.0 million, both well ahead of the third-place United States (USD 23.5 million). Total Indian turmeric exports that year reached USD 328 million across roughly 177,738 MT, and a separate trade-press estimate puts India's world market share at around 66%, making it the dominant global origin. UAE demand spans bulk importers supplying local repackaging and food-service operations as well as retail-focused buyers wanting finished, branded packs. This guide is for both: what to specify (curcumin, form, moisture), how UAE import requirements apply, and where first orders go wrong.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<!-- insight:buyer-segmentation -->
<h2>Why does the UAE pay more per kilo than Bangladesh for the same spice?</h2>
<p>The two markets are tied on value but not on volume. Dividing the WITS/UN Comtrade 2024 values by tonnage:</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table class="data-table">
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Market (2024)</th><th scope="col">Tonnes</th><th scope="col">Average unit value (USD/kg)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>United States</td><td>8,940</td><td>2.63</td></tr>
<tr><td>Germany</td><td>4,216</td><td>2.41</td></tr>
<tr><td>United Arab Emirates</td><td>27,902</td><td>1.83</td></tr>
<tr><td>Saudi Arabia</td><td>5,851</td><td>1.80</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bangladesh</td><td>36,198</td><td>1.38</td></tr>
<tr><td>India, all markets</td><td>177,738</td><td>1.85</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Bangladesh took about 30% more tonnage than the UAE for almost the same value, so its average was about 24% lower per kilo. The UAE sits almost exactly on India's world average, and well below the US and Germany. A unit value is an average across forms and grades, not a price, but the spread fits the buyers. Bangladesh's lower average points to mainly bulk, standard-grade whole turmeric. The UAE mixes three groups: Dubai traders who re-export across the Gulf, East Africa and beyond; spice grinders and packers supplying UAE retail; and food-service distributors. Higher-specification and nutraceutical-grade lots, which likely lift the US and German averages, are a smaller part of the UAE trade. Scenario: a Dubai trader buying Erode finger for re-export and a Sharjah packer buying ground turmeric for its own retail brand are both "UAE buyers", but they need different specifications and should not benchmark against each other.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<h2>What curcumin content should a UAE buyer specify?</h2>
<p>Turmeric's active compound, curcumin, is both the quality marker and the primary pricing lever. Standard culinary-grade turmeric runs at one curcumin level; buyers supplying pharmaceutical or nutraceutical manufacturing typically need higher, more tightly specified curcumin content and will pay accordingly. Tell us your end use, and we'll quote against the appropriate curcumin target rather than a generic grade.</p>

<h2>Which growing regions match your colour and curcumin target?</h2>
<p>We source from Salem and Erode in Tamil Nadu and Nizamabad in Telangana — three of India's most established turmeric-growing belts, each with somewhat different curcumin profiles and colour characteristics reflecting local soil and cultivar differences. If you have a preference or a specific past-supplier profile you're trying to match, tell us and we'll recommend the closest regional match. Buyers who know Sangli in Maharashtra, where the Rajapuri type is traded, should name it as a reference profile. After curing (boiling and drying), whole fingers are usually polished to remove the rough outer skin; how hard they are polished changes colour and appearance, so agree a reference sample.</p>

<h2>Should you buy bulk bags or private-label packs for the UAE?</h2>
<p>UAE buyers span both ends of the packaging spectrum — bulk importers supplying local repackaging and food-service operations, and retail-focused buyers wanting finished, branded packaging ready for supermarket shelves. Bulk typically moves in 25kg or 50kg bags at lower per-kilo cost; retail packaging (100g-1kg pouches or jars with your artwork) costs more per unit but captures more margin downstream if you have retail distribution. Tell us which model fits your business and we'll quote packaging options accordingly — see our <a href="/private-label-bulk-supply/">private label and bulk supply</a> page for how that process works.</p>

<!-- insight:container-economics -->
<h2>How much turmeric fits in a container to Jebel Ali?</h2>
<p>About 14-16 MT of bagged whole or cut turmeric in a 20ft and 24-26 MT in a 40ft. At the midpoints, 25 MT against 15 MT, a 40ft carries about 67% more, so it is the cheaper box per tonne whenever its freight rate to Jebel Ali is under about 1.67 times the 20ft rate. The low 20ft figure reflects bagged turmeric filling the box by volume before it reaches the weight limit, which is why the 40ft gains more here than it does for denser cargo.</p>
<p>At the 2024 UAE average of about USD 1.83 per kg, a 15 MT 20ft carries roughly USD 27,400 of turmeric, or 600 bags of 25kg. With 10-15 days ex-factory and 10-14 days to Jebel Ali, allow about four weeks from order to discharge, and add time for curing if you are buying from the new crop harvested January to March.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<!-- slot:docs -->

<!-- insight:documentation-failures -->
<p>MOCCAE handles agricultural import requirements at the federal level, while emirate-level food-control departments, such as Dubai Municipality in Dubai, register packaged food products — so the registration step applies to retail-packed turmeric, not just the consignment. Scenario: a Dubai importer orders 100g retail pouches in its own artwork. The bulk documents are complete, but the product was never registered with Dubai Municipality and the pouch label was not checked against UAE labelling rules, so the cartons wait in a Jebel Ali warehouse while the label is reworked. The same applies to halal: if your customers ask for halal documentation, the importer specifies the requirement and the certifying body it accepts, and that needs settling before production. Send label artwork and any registration or certification requirements with the order, and if the goods will be re-exported, name the final market so the label suits it.</p>
<!-- /insight -->
<p>For extraction and nutraceutical-grade lots, ask for the certificate of analysis to state the curcumin test method alongside the percentage. See our full <a href="/markets/uae/">UAE market guide</a> for ports, transit times and the broader documentation picture.</p>

<!-- price-behaviour:start -->
<h2>What actually determines the price you're quoted</h2>
<p>We don't quote turmeric off a flat per-kilo rate, because the number moves on several genuinely independent factors. Curcumin content is the biggest single lever — a 5%+ extraction-grade lot costs meaningfully more to produce and source than standard 2.5% culinary-grade material, since higher-curcumin rhizomes are a smaller share of any harvest. Form matters too: whole finger and mother bulb (prized specifically for extraction) command different pricing than cut/split or powder, which carries additional processing cost. Beyond spec, the Erode and Nizamabad market auction cycle moves with each new harvest (January-March harvest, curing over the following weeks, so prices typically firm up before the new crop clears the market and ease once fresh stock is available), the size of that year's crop across Telangana, Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu, and prevailing INR/AED exchange rates and Jebel Ali freight rates. Traders also watch the turmeric futures contract on NCDEX alongside the physical auctions.</p>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB, CIF or CFR against your specific form, curcumin target, moisture tolerance, packaging and destination port — never as a generic per-tonne rate. To get an accurate quote fast, tell us: form (finger/split/powder), target curcumin %, intended end use (culinary vs. extraction/nutraceutical), packaging preference, quantity and destination port.</p>
