<h2>What goes wrong on a first order, and how to avoid it</h2>
<p>The most common technical dispute on turmeric shipments is a curcumin-content mismatch between buyer and supplier labs. Curcumin is typically measured by spectrophotometric or HPLC methods, and results shift with sample preparation, moisture correction and instrument calibration — two labs testing the same lot can genuinely report different percentages without either being wrong. For extraction and nutraceutical-grade orders specifically, agree the test method and moisture-correction basis in your purchase order, not just a target percentage, and ask which method our certificate of analysis uses.</p>
<p>The second common issue is colour and moisture degradation from inadequate curing before bagging. Well-cured turmeric should run at or below 10-12% moisture with a deep, characteristic yellow-orange colour; rhizomes that were boiled and sun-dried too quickly or bagged before curing was complete can develop mould risk in transit or lose colour value on arrival. We cure fully before bagging as standard practice, and it's a fair question to ask any supplier: how long was this cured, and to what moisture level, before it left the warehouse?</p>
<p>Third, adulteration is a known industry-wide concern for turmeric specifically — historically, some supply chains have used lead chromate or other additives to boost colour artificially, which is both a food-safety and a regulatory risk for the importer. A proper certificate of analysis covering colour, curcumin and contaminant screening is the practical safeguard, and we'd recommend treating a supplier's willingness to provide detailed, method-specified lab documentation as a real signal, not a formality.</p>
<p>What first-time UAE buyers most often get wrong: assuming "turmeric" is a single commodity grade rather than a spec that varies meaningfully by form, curcumin content and curing quality. A quote without a stated curcumin target and form isn't comparable to one with — specify both upfront to get pricing you can actually compare across suppliers.</p>
<!-- operator-detail:end -->

